<article class="env-lemma" id="0FIN">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FIN">Lemma <span data-tag="0FIN">45.14.9</span>.</a> Assume given data (D0), (D1), and (D2') satisfying axioms (A1) – (A7). Let $p : P \to X$ be as in axiom (A3) with $X$ nonempty equidimensional. Then $\gamma $ commutes with pushforward along $p$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It suffices to prove this on generators for $\mathop{\mathrm{CH}}\nolimits _*(P)$. Thus it suffices to prove this for a cycle class of the form $\xi ^ i \cdot p^*\alpha $ where $0 \leq i \leq r - 1$ and $\alpha \in \mathop{\mathrm{CH}}\nolimits _*(X)$. Note that $p_*(\xi ^ i \cdot p^*\alpha ) = 0$ if $i < r - 1$ and $p_*(\xi ^{r - 1} \cdot p^*\alpha ) = \alpha $. On the other hand, we have $\gamma (\xi ^ i \cdot p^*\alpha ) = c^ i \cup p^*\gamma (\alpha )$ and by the projection formula (Lemma <a href="/tag/0FHB" data-tag="0FHB">45.9.1</a>) we have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  p_*\gamma (\xi ^ i \cdot p^*\alpha ) = p_*(c^ i) \cup \gamma (\alpha )  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Thus it suffices to show that $p_*c^ i = 0$ for $i < r - 1$ and $p_*c^{r - 1} = 1$. Equivalently, it suffices to prove that $\lambda _ P : H^{2d + 2r - 2}(P)(d + r - 1) \to F$ defined by the rules 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \lambda _ P(c^ i \cup p^*(a)) = \left\{  \begin{matrix}  0 

&  \text{if} 

&  i < r - 1 

\\  \int _ X(a) 

&  \text{if} 

&  i = r - 1 

\end{matrix} \right.  \]
</div>
    <p>
       satisfies the condition of axiom (A5). This follows from the computation of the class of the diagonal of $P$ in Lemma <a href="/tag/0FGR" data-tag="0FGR">45.6.2</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>